        <h1><a href="http://philbencomo.com/2010/09/basketball_in_china">篮球</a></h1>
        <p>I was 17 and playing basketball thousands of miles from home with kids whose halting English still made a mockery of my infantile Mandarin. But it didn&#8217;t matter; it never does.</p>

<p>It&#8217;s a hackneyed thread, about sports as a global language and cross-cultural connective tissue. It&#8217;s true, though.</p>

<p>What did they think of us? Two American teenagers, dribbling a basketball on an empty court outside a school in Beijing. Heads popped out of dormitory windows, from floors high and low, as if the students had been waiting for something to shatter the monotony of a languid mid-week afternoon. &#8220;What the hell?&#8221; they must have said, before rushing downstairs to play.</p>

<p>I most remember their faces, so exuberant and curious. We were just kids playing ball. Nothing else mattered.</p>

<p>Distant music flitted on the air as the sun set in China.</p>

